---
import Base from '../layouts/Base.astro'; import { COMMON } from '../lib/methodology.ts'; import { SCREENS } from '../lib/screens.ts'; import { RATES, CHARGES_VERSION } from '../lib/charges.ts'; import { STOP_PCT, NOTIONAL } from '../lib/ledger.ts'; import { ledger } from '../lib/data.ts'; import { dmy } from '../lib/view.ts';
const L = ledger();
---
<Base title="Methodology" reading description="How every figure on Rulebook is made: one ledger, one accounting method, every charge counted once.">
<div class="wrap hero prose"><span class="label eyebrow">Methodology</span><h1>How every number is made.</h1><p class="lede">One ledger is the only source of the public figures. Pages format it; they never compute their own version.</p></div>
<div class="wrap prose"><h2>The ledger</h2><p>A single record, rebuilt each trading night, holds the screens, every weekly run, every pick, every paper trade, every void, and one book snapshot per day. History is append-only: a new night can add rows, never rewrite old ones. A test fails the build if it tries. Ledger as of {dmy(L.as_of)}.</p>
<h2 data-u="mt6">The accounting</h2><ul><li><strong>Realised</strong> is what exits have booked, partial exits included, after their share of charges.</li><li><strong>Unrealised</strong> is the remaining quantity against its remaining cost, after the buy charges that belong to it.</li><li><strong>Every charge is counted once</strong>, attached to one fill.</li><li><strong>R</strong> is measured against the original position's risk, set at entry and not changed by partial exits.</li></ul>
<p>Worked example, before charges: buy 100 at ₹100 with a stop at ₹90. Sell 50 at ₹120. Mark the other 50 at ₹110. Realised ₹1,000. Unrealised ₹500. Total ₹1,500. Initial risk ₹1,000. Total result 1.5R. The site's tests run this example every time.</p>
<h2 data-u="mt6">Picks become simulated trades</h2><p>{COMMON.mechanics} Each position is ₹{NOTIONAL.toLocaleString('en-IN')}; the stop is {STOP_PCT}% below entry.</p>
<h2 data-u="mt6">Charges</h2><p>Schedule {CHARGES_VERSION}: delivery equity. Securities transaction tax {RATES.sttPct}% on both sides, exchange transaction charge {RATES.exchangePct}%, SEBI fee {RATES.sebiPct}%, stamp duty {RATES.stampBuyPct}% on buys, GST {RATES.gstPct}% on brokerage, exchange and SEBI fees, and a flat depository charge of ₹{RATES.dpSellFlat} on sells. Brokerage is set to zero. Rates change; a change would create a new schedule version and be recorded.</p>
<h2 data-u="mt6">Sample size</h2><p>Every statistic shows the number of trades behind it. Under 30 closed trades, the statistic carries the label "too few to conclude anything". The record starts empty and takes months to reach that size.</p>
<h2 data-u="mt6">Benchmark</h2><p>The Nifty 500 price index, which excludes dividends. The simulated trades also exclude dividends, so the two are compared on the same basis. Total-return data was not available from a reliable free source.</p>
<h2 data-u="mt6">The screens</h2><ul>{SCREENS.map((s) => <li><a href={`/screens/${s.id}/methodology/`}>{s.name}</a></li>)}</ul>
<h2 data-u="mt6">What we cannot see</h2><p>Real trades have slippage, partial fills, taxes on gains and halts that this simulation ignores or simplifies. Fills are modelled at the next session's open. Data comes from an unofficial source. These limits make the simulated record more flattering than a real one would be.</p></div></Base>
